<Base
  title="About"
  description="Abhinav Gupta is a full-stack software engineer building backend platforms and browser tooling, with security experience. Two years at SquareX and Zscaler, now looking at architecture roles."
>
  <header class="page-head">
    <h1>About</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      I’m a full-stack engineer who ended up spending most of his time inside a
      browser. Backend services, auth, data pipelines, Chromium patches,
      extension code: if it sat between a person and something that could hurt
      them, I probably had my hands in it. Next, I’d like to spend more time
      designing systems like these, not just keeping them alive.
    </p>
  </header>
  <div class="prose cv">
    <h2>Zscaler <span class="meta">Feb 2026 – Jul 2026</span></h2>
    <p>
      Zscaler bought SquareX, and I came along with the furniture. Same work,
      much longer customer list. I rebuilt the pipeline that keeps our Chromium
      fork’s patches applying on top of new upstream releases; it has carried
      the browser through seven major Chromium versions. I also shipped 56+
      custom Chrome APIs for device compliance and threat detection, rewrote
      the extension’s pattern matcher so it answers in under a millisecond and
      handles twice the traffic, trimmed 30% off per-tab memory, and ported the
      whole protection stack to Firefox on Android.
    </p>

    <h2>SquareX <span class="meta">Jun 2024 – Feb 2026</span></h2>
    <p>
      I showed up as a summer intern and never quite left. I built backend and
      browser-protection features used by 80+ companies and 40,000+ people at
      once: the OIDC login layer, a data-leak-protection service that reads
      Office and PDF files (later ported to WASM, because it needed to be
      faster), and the Zero Trust Browser’s UI and content isolation inside
      Chromium. I also co-created <a href="https://browser.security">browser.security</a>,
      an open-source set of 30+ tricks that slip past web security gateways.
      We showed it at DEF CON, and Forbes wrote about it.
    </p>

    <h2>On the side</h2>
    <p>
      Apps, libraries and research projects, each with the decision that made
      it interesting, are <a href="/#decisions-h">on the home page</a>. I also
      keep <a href="/dsa">notes on every DSA problem I solve</a> and
      a <a href="/lab">lab</a> with two small games about caches and latency.
    </p>

    <h2>Tools</h2>
    <p>
      I think in C/C++, Go, Rust, Python and TypeScript. I reach for Kafka,
      Redis, PostgreSQL, GCP and Kubernetes, and I know more about Chromium and
      Firefox internals than is strictly healthy.
    </p>

    <h2>School</h2>
    <p>B.Tech in Computer Science and Engineering from IIT Tirupati.</p>
  </div>
